              "text": "{\n  \"query_text\": \"For adults and children aiming to reduce their risk of cardiovascular disease, stroke, coronary heart disease, or to control blood pressure, how should the use of lower-sodium salt substitutes (LSSS) be managed within WHO's overall recommended daily sodium intake limits, and what population-level safety conditions must be considered?\",\n  \"target_population\": [\n    \"adults\",\n    \"children\"\n  ],\n  \"conditions\": [\n    \"high blood pressure\",\n    \"cardiovascular disease\",\n    \"stroke\",\n    \"coronary heart disease\",\n    \"undiagnosed conditions where increased potassium intake is not advisable\"\n  ],\n  \"draft_rationale\": \"Answering this query requires integrating the 2012 WHO recommendations on sodium reduction targets (<2 g/day for adults, adjusted downward for children) and associated health conditions (blood pressure, CVD, stroke, coronary heart disease) with the 2025 WHO guideline on LSSS. Specifically, it requires explaining that LSSS must be used such that the sodium obtained is less than the regular salt replaced to help meet these overall limits, while noting the conditional nature of the LSSS recommendation due to potential harms in settings with undiagnosed conditions where increased potassium is contraindicated.\"\n}",
